From 4dbaf8350f6dc687e6be2e6e05056cdaf07e5af0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 23:35:33 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] ForkGuest: copy a vfork/CLONE_VM child's memory back to
 the parent

vfork(2) and glibc's posix_spawn (clone(CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK)) run the
child in a copy of the address space. Sharing it for real was reverted:
the child's FEX code mutates the shared heap (see the history above
ForkGuest). So nothing the child wrote before execve or _exit reached the
parent:
- posix_spawn of a missing program returned 0, with a child exiting 127,
  instead of the exec errno. glibc's child stores that errno in memory
  shared with the parent.
- A vfork child's variable writes were lost.

Now the parent copies the memory back:
- The parent keeps waiting on the vfork pipe.
- The child calls VForkChildSync just before execve (both ExecveHandler
  paths) and in exit/exit_group. It sends a byte and waits for an ack on a
  second close-on-exec pipe.
- The parent reads the child's memory with process_vm_readv (allowed for a
  descendant under Yama ptrace_scope 1). For each private, writable,
  non-executable guest VMA it copies every host page that differs from its
  own. Having been blocked since the clone, any difference is the child's
  write.
- A successful execve closes the pipes and ends the wait.

The vfork syscall now passes CLONE_VM as well (CloneFork strips it before
the host clone), so vfork(2) takes this path too.

Limits:
- The copy-back is skipped when the parent has more than one guest thread,
  since another thread's writes to the same pages would be undone.
- Mappings the child creates or removes are not reflected.
- Executable writable mappings are skipped because of SMC protection.
- A child killed by a signal before execve/_exit is not synced.

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, FEX_PORTABLE=1, a static x86-64
test:
  before: posix_spawn missing: ret=0;      vfork child write seen=no
  after:  posix_spawn missing: ret=ENOENT; vfork child write seen=yes
Linux (the same test on an aarch64 Raspberry Pi 5): ENOENT, yes. Ubuntu
24.04 bash from the RootFS still runs `(exit 4) & wait $!` and $(...).

Found in POWERarm (A64Syscalls sys_process), where GCC's driver spawns its
tools with posix_spawn.
